<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eleazar López Contreras</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eleazar López Contreras was a Venezuelan army officer and politician who was president of Venezuela from 1935 to 1941. Minister of war under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-vicente-gomez">Juan Vicente Gómez</a>, he succeeded the dictator on his death and began a cautious political opening, allowing greater freedom of the press and of organisation while keeping firm control, and handed power to his chosen successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaias-medina-angarita">Isaías Medina Angarita</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eleazar-lopez-contreras.jpg" alt="Eleazar López Contreras" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eleazar_L%C3%B3pez_Contreras.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Eleazar López Contreras was born in Queniquea, Venezuela, on 5 May 1883, to Manuel María López Trejo and María Catalina Contreras Mora. His father died of malaria a few days after his birth, and his uncle Fernando María Contreras, a priest, became a father figure to him.</p><p>López supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cipriano-castro">Cipriano Castro</a> in the Restorative Liberal Revolution and was wounded at the Battle of Tocuyito on 14 September 1899. Castro made him his aide-de-camp. He served as Minister of War under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-vicente-gomez">Juan Vicente Gómez</a> from 1931 to 1935.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Gómez entered a coma on 15 December 1935 and died two days later, after 27 years in power. López was his designated successor, and the cabinet voted to make him acting president to avoid a vacuum of power. The Congress elected him two weeks later to serve the rest of Gómez's term, and on 25 April 1936 it voted him a full term by 132 votes to 1. In February 1936 he announced a February Program promoting labour rights, education, better management of public funds, modernisation of agriculture and economic development, and a labour law followed that year.</p><p>On 2 January 1936 he announced that La Rotunda, the main political prison, would be demolished. He nationalised the large fortune Gómez had acquired in July 1936, although he had first opposed it and allowed over 90 of Gómez's relatives to leave the country with money. Petroleum workers began a strike in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maracaibo">Maracaibo</a> region on 11 December 1936, which López ended by decree on 22 January 1937; oil production fell by 39% during it. In 1938 he ended oil concessions to private persons. In 1939 he accepted for Venezuela the ships Koenigstein and Caribia, which had fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>.</p><p>The constitution of 1936 restricted voting to literate men over 21, though over half of Venezuelans could not read, and in 1937 the Venezuelan Organizing Movement, the Progressive Republican Party and other left-wing groups were banned. López supported gradual democratisation while fearing competitive politics, and he was ideologically conservative. He cut the presidential term from seven years to five. He considered letting a civilian succeed him, but his military colleagues persuaded him otherwise, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaias-medina-angarita">Isaías Medina Angarita</a>, his Minister of War, succeeded him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> was overthrown in 1945, and both he and López were arrested and sent into exile. López stayed abroad even after an invitation to return in 1947 and came back only in 1951. He married three times and had eight children.He died on 2 January 1973.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eleazar López Contreras” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his death the governments of <b>Eleazar López Contreras</b> and Isaías Medina Angarita began a cautious opening, and the Hydrocarbons Law of 1943 raised the state’s share of oil profits.</blockquote><a href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tachira">Táchira</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/venezuela">Venezuela</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/venezuela">Venezuela</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=7.83,-72.08&amp;year=1941" aria-label="Táchira on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2557 1871 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Eleazar López Contreras"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3007 2141h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3007 2141h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=7.83,-72.08&amp;year=1941">What was here in 1941? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
